at national heroes square once known as  trafalgar square change was again coming  the prince of wales arriving to witness  the ceremony as barbados said goodbye to  the queen  this was the final time after nearly 400  years that a royal would oversee a  military parade in this way  the moment had come  the queen's standard was lowered and  then the countdown to midnight  barbados no longer a realm now a  republic  [Applause]  the new president dame sandra mason was  sworn in  to pledge your allegiance was prime  minister mia motley who's pushed so hard  for this new stage of independence  as those ties were cut  prince charles formally acknowledged the  pain of the past  from the darkest days  of our past  and the appalling atrocity of slavery  which forever stains our history  the people of this island  forged their path with extraordinary  fortitude  but for those who've waited so long for  this they still want more  we want  tangible  actions towards reparations for our  people  for our nation and our country we have  we have endured a lot we have still  enjoyed the legacy of the enslaved  period covid meant that large crowds  were kept out and many are still divided  about what this really means  i think there should have been a public  vote  and i think it should have been decided  by the people and not by a few mps  everybody's out  celebrating  feeling proud  the royal family have always said this  was a decision for the people of  barbados after all they have been  independent since the 1960s and the  queen's role has largely been ceremonial  but this certainly is not the royal  family entirely stepping away they will  still be front and center when it comes  to maintaining those connections and  shaping that new relationship  rihanna was a surprise guest  this is a new chapter described by some  as a full stop on their colonial past  now two women at the helm writing their  future  rhiannon mills sky news in bridgetown  [Music]
 

